    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d282">

                <pb facs="0453" n="339" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg339"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.428/804</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Howard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-05-16"><hi rendition="#italic">May 16,
                        1924</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 439</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honour to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of the 12th instant, regarding the Northern Pacific Halibut
                    Fishery, in which you were good enough to inform me that as soon as he is
                    informed that the Canadian Parliament has passed the legislation, which it has
                    lately been considering on this subject, the Chairman of the Committee on
                    Foreign Relations proposes to resubmit to the Senate the Convention referred to
                    it by the President on December 11th, 1923.</p>
                <p>I am happy to be in a position to inform you that the bill, of which I have the
                    honour to enclose a copy,<anchor n="7" xml:id="d282-f1"/> has been passed by
                    both Houses of the Canadian Parliament, by the House of Commons on the 3rd
                    ultimo and by the Senate on the 10th ultimo, and that it only requires the
                    formal assent of His Excellency the Governor-General (which can and will be
                    given at any convenient time) before it becomes law. There appears to be
                    therefore no reason why the Chairman of the Committee on Foreign Relations
                    should <pb facs="0454" n="340" xml:id="pg340"/> not now proceed to secure the
                    necessary action by the Senate and I shall be glad to be informed as soon as
                    this action has been taken.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>(For the Ambassador:)<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. G.
                            Chilton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
